# WordPress Abilities API

Ahentic should expose and consume WordPress capabilities through the **Abilities API** — the machine-readable registry of actions for AI agents, automation, REST, and UI triggers.

## Reference docs

- [Abilities API in WordPress 6.9](https://make.wordpress.org/core/2025/11/10/abilities-api-in-wordpress-6-9/)
- [Client-Side Abilities API in WordPress 7.0](https://make.wordpress.org/core/2026/03/24/client-side-abilities-api-in-wordpress-7-0/)
- [Using Abilities To Build AI in WordPress](https://j.cv/using-abilities-to-build-ai-in-wordpress/)

## Why Abilities

An **ability** is a named unit of functionality with:

- label / description
- category
- input + output JSON Schema
- permission callback
- execute callback

Register once → discover via PHP/REST/JS → reuse for sparkle actions, agent tool loops, MCP, workflows.

Prefer Abilities over buried AJAX handlers, one-off REST routes, or private tool registries when the action should be agent- or automation-facing.

## Server-side (PHP) — WordPress 6.9+

Register categories on `wp_abilities_api_categories_init` and abilities on `wp_abilities_api_init`.

```php
add_action( 'wp_abilities_api_init', 'ahentic_register_abilities' );

function ahentic_register_abilities() {
	wp_register_ability(
		'ahentic/example-action',
		array(
			'label'               => __( 'Example action', 'ahentic' ),
			'description'         => __( 'Does something useful on the site.', 'ahentic' ),
			'category'            => 'ahentic',
			'input_schema'        => array( /* draft-04 schema */ ),
			'output_schema'       => array( /* draft-04 schema */ ),
			'execute_callback'    => 'ahentic_run_example_action',
			'permission_callback' => static function () {
				return current_user_can( 'manage_options' );
			},
			'meta'                => array(
				'show_in_rest' => true,
			),
		)
	);
}
```

Useful PHP APIs:

- `wp_register_ability()` / `wp_unregister_ability()` / `wp_has_ability()` / `wp_get_ability()` / `wp_get_abilities()`
- Category helpers: `wp_register_ability_category()`, etc.
- Hooks: `wp_before_execute_ability`, `wp_after_execute_ability`
- REST (when exposed): namespace `wp-abilities/v1` — list/get abilities & categories; `…/abilities/{name}/run`

Ability names: namespaced (`ahentic/...`), lowercase with dashes.

## Client-side (JS) — WordPress 7.0+

Two packages ([dev note](https://make.wordpress.org/core/2026/03/24/client-side-abilities-api-in-wordpress-7-0/)):

| Package | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `@wordpress/abilities` | Pure store: register, query, execute (no server fetch) |
| `@wordpress/core-abilities` | Loads server abilities from REST into the store |

Enqueue when Ahentic needs server abilities:

```php
wp_enqueue_script_module( '@wordpress/core-abilities' );
```

Core already enqueues `@wordpress/core-abilities` on admin pages in WP 7.0 — still enqueue explicitly if we depend on it on the front-end or must guarantee load order.

```js
import {
	registerAbility,
	registerAbilityCategory,
	getAbilities,
	executeAbility,
} from '@wordpress/abilities'

// Or dynamic: await import( '@wordpress/abilities' )
```

- Register client-only abilities (navigate, insert blocks, UI side-effects) with `registerAbility` + `callback`.
- Run any ability (client or server) via `executeAbility( name, input )`.
- Handle errors: `ability_permission_denied`, `ability_invalid_input`, `ability_invalid_output`.
- Use `@wordpress/data` + `store as abilitiesStore` (`core/abilities`) for reactive lists in React.

Annotations (meta): `readonly`, `destructive`, `idempotent` — affect REST method selection for server abilities.

## AI integration patterns (for Ahentic)

From [j.cv](https://j.cv/using-abilities-to-build-ai-in-wordpress/):

1. **Single-shot** — one UI trigger → one `executeAbility` (or PHP `WP_Ability::execute`).
2. **Agentic loops** — LLM (AI Client) chooses which abilities to call; abilities are the tool surface.
3. **Deterministic workflows** — fixed graph; AI may live inside some nodes; abilities remain the callable units.

Ahentic agent/runtime tooling should map to Abilities where practical, so the same actions work for the sidebar agent, REST, and future MCP/WebMCP adapters.

## Do / Don’t

- **Do** register Ahentic site actions as abilities with schemas + permission checks.
- **Do** keep React focused on UX; execute abilities through the official client/PHP APIs.
- **Don’t** call WordPress mutate APIs ad-hoc from the LLM layer without going through an ability (or an intentional internal layer that will become one).
- **Don’t** invent a parallel public tool registry that duplicates Abilities.
